DOMAIN_OVERVIEW:
The central dispute in this domain concerns whether Hegel's philosophy
requires robust metaphysical commitments — specifically, a form of
absolute idealism in which Geist is the fundamental ontological
category — or whether it can be "deflated" into a post-Kantian,
social-normative account of self-determining rationality. The
"non-metaphysical" or "neo-Kantian" reading associated with Robert
Pippin and Terry Pinkard construes Hegel's talk of Geist as a
sophisticated account of the conditions for human rational agency and
self-consciousness, not a claim about cosmic mind. Frederick Beiser
mounted influential resistance to this reading, arguing that it
evacuates Hegel's genuine speculative commitments. Robert Stern
occupies a mediating position, defending a revisionary metaphysics
that is neither pre-critical dogmatism nor mere transcendental
idealism.

Pippin's 1989 "Hegel's Idealism" established the neo-Kantian paradigm,
arguing that the Logic's categories are conditions of possible
experience rather than claims about mind-independent reality. His 2008
"Hegel's Practical Philosophy" extended this to practical reason,
treating free will as rational self-governance grounded in social
recognition. His 2018 "Hegel's Realm of Shadows" revisited the Logic
more fully, arguing that it is a metaphysics of conceptuality but not
of substance. Beiser's responses (1995, 2005) insist that Hegel's
holism about nature and Geist requires genuine ontological idealism.
James Kreines has since argued that both camps misread Hegel by
importing too much Kantian vocabulary, proposing a "metaphysics without
pre-critical monism."

On free will specifically, Hegel presents freedom not as a bare
property of individual agents but as a achievement of rational
self-determination within ethical life — a position that can be read as
a form of compatibilism. The unity of theoretical and practical spirit
(Encyclopaedia §481) grounds this: willing is not distinct from
knowing but is knowing as self-determining. Houlgate, Hegel's Ethical
Thought (Wood), and the Beiser Hegel monograph all elaborate this
connection between the Logic's account of the Concept and the practical
philosophy.

RELEVANCE_TO_PROJECT:
This domain directly underpins the project's core question: what Hegel
means by calling freedom the "truth" of spirit, and whether this
depends on absolute idealism or can be reformulated in social-normative
terms. The Pippin/Beiser debate is the central scholarly fault-line
around which the broader project's claims about Hegel's positive
account of freedom must be situated.

NOTABLE_GAPS:
Detailed treatments of the Science of Logic's Doctrine of the Concept
as a theory of self-determination — as distinct from Pippin's
broad-brush reading — remain relatively sparse in article-length form;
the Houlgate (2006) book on the Logic partly addresses this but is not
primarily focused on freedom. The link between the Logic's analysis of
necessity-as-sublated-freedom and the practical philosophy is
underexplored at article length.

SYNTHESIS_GUIDANCE:
The Pippin/Beiser/Stern triangle should anchor the section on
metaphysical presuppositions; Kreines 2006 provides a useful
third-party diagnosis. The Houlgate (1995) article and Donoghue (2021)
bridge to the free will question directly.

KEY_POSITIONS:
- Non-metaphysical (neo-Kantian): 4 papers — Pippin 1989, 2008, 2018; Pinkard 1996
- Robust metaphysical idealism: 3 papers — Beiser 1995, 2005; Winfield 1988
- Mediating/revisionary metaphysics: 2 papers — Stern 2009; Kreines 2006
- Free will and spirit: 3 papers — Houlgate 1995, 2006; Donoghue 2021
- Background/context: 1 paper — Wood 1990
====================================================================
}

